Source System Analysis — Child-Simple Study Notes
1. What is source system analysis?

Source system analysis means:

Before extracting data, we study the source system to understand:

what the system does

why the business uses it

what data is inside it

where the data is stored

how we can access it

how much data is there

whether we need full load or incremental load

whether extraction will affect performance

whether we have permission to access it

Simple meaning

It means:

“Before taking data, first understand the system properly.”

Very simple example

If someone gives you a box and asks you to take things from it, first you ask:

what is this box?

who owns it?

what is inside it?

can I open it?

can I take everything?

will it break if I use it?

Source system analysis is the same idea.

Area 1: Business Context and Ownership

This is the first area to understand.

Because before touching tables, you must know:

what the system does for the business

who owns the data

what problem the system solves

A. Who owns the data?
Simple meaning

This means:

Who is responsible for this data?
Who takes care of it?

It may be:

business team

application team

DBA team

vendor team

product owner

Why this matters

Because the data owner helps us:

understand what the data means

clarify doubts

confirm if data is correct

approve access

inform us about schema changes

Example

vehicle registration data → DMV operations team

billing data → finance team

Child-simple meaning

Data owner = the person or team responsible for the data and taking care of it.

Interview line

“We first identify the data owner because they help validate business meaning, approve access, and clarify any issues in the source data.”

B. What business process does it support?
Simple meaning

This means:

What is this system used for?
Or:

What business problem is this system solving?

Examples:

customer onboarding

vehicle registration

claims processing

order management

inspections

payments

Why this matters

Because data makes sense only when you understand the business process.

Example

Suppose a table has:

status = A

Without business context, you may think:

A = Active

But in that system, it may actually mean:

A = Approved

So business understanding gives the true meaning of data.

Child-simple meaning

This teaches us why the system exists and what work it does for the business.

Interview line

“We understand what business process the source system supports so we can interpret the data correctly.”

C. System and data documentation
Simple meaning

Ask whether documents already exist.

Examples:

source system overview document

ETL mapping document

table descriptions

column descriptions

API documentation

business glossary

source-to-target mapping

data dictionary

Why this matters

Because documentation saves time.

Instead of guessing, documentation tells us:

which tables are important

what each column means

which values are valid

which tables are active

which tables are obsolete

how extraction should happen

If documentation is missing

Then we depend more on:

SMEs

DBAs

data owners

data profiling

Child-simple meaning

Documentation helps us understand the system and the columns/fields inside it.

Interview line

“Documentation helps us understand table purpose, column meaning, relationships, and extraction rules faster and more accurately.”

D. Data model and data catalog
1. Data model
Simple meaning

It shows:

How tables are related

Examples:

customer table linked to address table

vehicle table linked to inspection table

order table linked to order items table

Why this matters

Because it helps us understand:

parent-child relationships

joins

primary keys

foreign keys

master tables

transaction tables

Child-simple meaning

Data model teaches how tables are connected.

2. Data catalog
Simple meaning

A data catalog is like an inventory or index of data assets.

It tells:

table names

column names

definitions

owners

data types

tags

sensitivity

Why this matters

Because it helps us:

find the right data quickly

reduce confusion

support governance

understand available data assets

Child-simple meaning

Data catalog teaches what data is available and what it means.

Interview line

“The data model helps us understand table relationships, while the data catalog helps us discover and interpret available data assets.”

Area 2: Architecture and Technology Stack

Now we move from business understanding to technical understanding.

Now we ask:

Where is the data stored, and how can we access it?

A. How is data stored?
Simple meaning

This means:

Which technology stores the data?

Examples:

SQL Server

Oracle

PostgreSQL

MySQL

MongoDB

AWS RDS

Azure SQL

flat files

data lake

SaaS application

Why this matters

Because extraction method depends on storage type.

Examples

Oracle → DMS, direct query, CDC

SQL Server → CDC, full extract

API system → no direct table access

files → CSV or JSON extract

Kafka → streaming events

So storage type decides

extraction approach

tools

authentication

performance planning

Child-simple meaning

This teaches where the data is stored and what technology is used to keep it.

Interview line

“We identify the storage technology because the extraction method and integration design depend on where and how the source data is stored.”

B. What are the integration capabilities?
Simple meaning

This means:

How can the system share data with us?

Possible ways:

direct database connection

API

Kafka / event stream

file extracts

CDC

webhooks

vendor export

replication tools like DMS

Why this matters

Because not every source allows direct DB access.

Examples

some systems only allow API

some send daily CSV files

some support CDC

some publish events to Kafka

some allow read-only DB connection

Child-simple meaning

Integration capabilities means the ways the source system can give data to us.

Interview line

“We assess the source system’s integration capabilities to decide whether data should be extracted through direct DB access, APIs, files, or event-based methods.”

Area 3: Extract and Load Planning

This is where we decide:

How extraction will happen in real life

This is very important.

A. Incremental vs Full Load
Full load
Meaning

Take all records every time

Example

Read full customer table of 10 million rows again.

Use when

first-time load

small table

no change tracking available

Problems

slow

heavy on source

expensive

repeats same data

Incremental load
Meaning

Take only new or changed records

Example

Only rows updated after yesterday.

Use when

daily or regular loads

large tables

source supports timestamp / CDC / change tracking

Benefits

faster

less load

efficient

Child-simple meaning

Full load = take everything
Incremental load = take only new or changed data

Interview line

“We determine whether extraction should be full or incremental based on source capabilities, data volume, and business needs.”

B. Data scope and historical needs
Simple meaning

This means:

How much data do we really need?

Questions:

do we need all tables or only some?

do we need all columns or only important ones?

do we need only current data or history also?

how many years of history are required?

Why this matters

Because the business may not need everything.

Example

Source has 500 tables, but only 40 may matter for the warehouse.

History examples

3 years of claims history

full customer history

only active records

daily snapshots

Child-simple meaning

This teaches what data we need and how much old data we need.

Interview line

“We define data scope and history requirements so we extract only relevant data and meet reporting or compliance needs.”

C. What is the expected size of extracts?
Simple meaning

This means:

How big is the data?

Ask:

row counts

daily growth

file sizes

changed rows per day

largest tables

average extract size

Why this matters

Because size affects:

network

storage

job duration

partitioning

performance

tool selection

Example

small table → 50,000 rows

large table → 2 billion rows

daily CDC → 2 million rows

Child-simple meaning

This teaches how much data we are going to pull and whether our system can handle it.

Interview line

“We estimate extract size to plan infrastructure, job design, scheduling, and performance optimization.”

D. Are there data volume limitations?
Simple meaning

This means:

Can the source handle large reads, or are there restrictions?

Examples:

API allows only 10,000 records per call

extraction allowed only at night

source cannot handle heavy full-table scans

vendor gives only weekly export

network bandwidth is limited

Why this matters

Because even if data exists, we may not be allowed to pull too much too fast.

Child-simple meaning

This teaches whether the source has limits on how much data we can pull and how safely we should extract it.

Interview line

“We identify any source-side or interface limitations so extraction can be designed safely and within operational boundaries.”

E. How to avoid impacting source system performance?
Simple meaning

Source systems are usually operational systems.
Their main job is to support business users, not our ETL.

If extraction is too heavy:

app becomes slow

users complain

DBA team blocks the job

Ways to avoid impact

use incremental loads

use CDC instead of full scans

schedule off-hours extraction

read from replica / read-only copy

extract only needed columns

extract only needed rows

batch the data

avoid complex joins on source

coordinate with DBA

Child-simple meaning

This teaches how to take data without making the source system slow.

Interview line

“We design extraction carefully to avoid affecting source system performance, usually by using incremental loads, off-hours scheduling, limited queries, or CDC-based approaches.”

F. Authentication and authorization
Authentication
Meaning

How do we prove who we are?

Examples:

username/password

IAM role

service account

token

certificate

Authorization
Meaning

What are we allowed to access?

Examples:

read-only access

only selected tables

no PII access

API scope restrictions

Why this matters

Because without correct access:

we cannot connect

we may access wrong data

security rules may be broken

Child-simple meaning

Authentication = who are you?

Authorization = what are you allowed to access?

Interview line

“We confirm authentication and authorization requirements to ensure secure and approved access to the source system.”

Final Easy Summary
Why do we do source system analysis?

Because before extracting data, we must understand:

who owns the data

what business problem the system solves

what documentation exists

how tables are related

what data is available

where data is stored

how data can be shared

whether we should do full or incremental load

how much data we need

how big the extracts are

whether there are limitations

how to avoid affecting source performance

what access is needed

One-line final meaning

Source system analysis means understanding the business, data, technology, extraction options, limitations, and security before building the pipeline.

Short Interview Answer

“Source system analysis is the process of understanding a source before extracting data from it. We study the business purpose, data owner, documentation, table relationships, storage technology, integration options, data volume, load strategy, performance constraints, and access rules. This helps us extract the right data in the right way without affecting the source system.”

Ultra-Simple Revision Sheet
Area 1: Business context and ownership

who owns the data?

what business process does it support?

what documents are available?

how are tables related?

what data assets exist?

Area 2: Architecture and technology stack

where is data stored?

what technology is used?

how can the source share data?

Area 3: Extract and load planning

full load or incremental?

what data is needed?

how much history is needed?

how big is the extract?

are there any limits?

how do we avoid source impact?

what access is needed?
